 ## Background
-Compression Domain Rendering was proposed to address the lack of attenuation and occlusion in frequency domain rendering while achieving high compression ratios and fast rendering speed [S2, unofficial]. Chiueh et al. applied 3D Hartley transform to extend JPEG for subcubes, performing frequency domain rendering on subcubes before compositing sub-images in the spatial domain [S2, unofficial]. Ning and Hesselink applied vector quantization in the spatial domain to compress volume and directly rendered quantized blocks [S2, unofficial]. Fowler and Yagel combined differential pulse-code modulation and Huffman coding for lossless compression [S2, unofficial]. Yeo and Liu applied discrete cosine transform based compression on overlapping blocks [S2, unofficial]. The specific hardware-accelerated texture decoding method described in [S1, unofficial] utilizes RGB index textures and dependent texture fetches to reconstruct VQ-encoded data on the GPU.
+Compression Domain Rendering was proposed to address the lack of attenuation and occlusion in frequency domain rendering while achieving high compression ratios and fast rendering speed [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Chiueh et al. applied 3D Hartley transform to extend JPEG for subcubes, performing frequency domain rendering on subcubes before compositing sub-images in the spatial domain [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Ning and Hesselink applied vector quantization in the spatial domain to compress volume and directly rendered quantized blocks [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Fowler and Yagel combined differential pulse-code modulation and Huffman coding for lossless compression [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Yeo and Liu applied discrete cosine transform based compression on overlapping blocks [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Guthe et al. and Sohn and Bajaj used principles from MPEG encoding to render time-varying datasets in the compression domain [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. The specific hardware-accelerated texture decoding method described in [S1, unofficial] utilizes RGB index textures and dependent texture fetches to reconstruct VQ-encoded data on the GPU.
 
